### Signaling RSVP-TE Tunnels on an SR-MPLS Forwarding Plane Using Adjacency Segment Identifiers
Presented by Pawan Pillamari ([3. Signaling RSVP-TE Tunnels on an SR-MPLS Forwarding Plane Using Adjacency Segment Identifiers](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-mpls-3-signaling-rsvp-te-tunnels-on-an-sr-mpls-forwarding-plane-using-adjacency-segment-identifiers-01))

* **Overview:** Target use case is for operators running SR-MPLS who require a distributed bandwidth engineering toolkit using RSVP-TE signaling. Replaces the tailing labels in RFC 8577 with IGP-advertised SR Adjacency SIDs.
* **Technical Details:**
  * Path computation occurs at the ingress node or PCE. Per-hop admission control and bandwidth reservations are signaled via RSVP-TE path/resv messages, maintaining RSVP control plane state without adding state to the forwarding plane.
  * Employs automatic delegation and existing SR Adjacency SIDs/binding sets. Coexistence with SR native traffic is managed via static bandwidth partitioning (RFC 8426) or dedicated adjacency sets.
  * Protection supports both signaled and non-signaled backup paths (e.g., TI-LFA).
* **Discussion:**
  * Andrew Stone requested a clear, overarching architecture document detailing how RSVP-TE interacts with SR across spring and MPLS drafts.
  * Adrian Farrel noted that distributing bandwidth reservation control for both standard MPLS-TE and SR-MPLS over shared links offers significant efficiency over partitioning bandwidth between centralized and distributed control planes.
  * Himanshu Shah and a Huawei representative asked about per-LSP state and bandwidth management at transit routers without dedicated data plane labels. Pawan Pillamari and Tony Przygienda clarified that bandwidth tracking and preemption occur strictly in the control plane per LSP, regardless of label aggregation in the data plane.
  * Zafar Ali questioned the necessity of using SR data plane SIDs without local labels and stressed the importance of transit per-hop link accounting.

