### Support ECN in MPLS Using MPLS Network Actions
Presented by Joel Halpern ([4. Support ECN in MPLS Using MPLS Network Actions](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-mpls-support-ecn-in-mpls-using-mpls-network-actions-00))

* **Overview:** Proposes a mechanism using MPLS Network Actions (MNA) to allow simultaneous support for DiffServ, ECN, and MPLS without stealing DiffServ code points.
* **Technical Details:**
  * Defines a new MNA opcode carrying the 2-bit ECN field, supported in both in-stack and post-stack MNA formats.
  * At ingress, ECN-capable IP packets are encapsulated with the MNA ECN opcode. Transit nodes detecting congestion set the ECN bits per RFC 6040 procedures. Egress nodes decapsulate the header and propagate the ECN marking to the inner IP packet.
* **Discussion:**
  * Tarek Saad asked how DiffServ and ECN interact. Joel Halpern clarified that DiffServ and ECN remain completely independent fields within the MNA structure.
  * Li Zhang raised concerns regarding legacy routers that do not support MNA ECN. Joel Halpern explained that non-supporting nodes simply ignore the MNA opcode and forward the packet normally; congestion experienced at non-supporting nodes will not be marked, but overall forwarding remains unaffected. Operational considerations text will be added.
  * Tony Przygienda noted that this capability is highly relevant for AI data center networks requiring urgent ECN support over MPLS.

